------------------------------------------this keyword----------------------------------------------
📌 What is this keyword?

this is a reference variable in Java.
It always refers to the current object of the class.
Automatically passed to non‑static methods and constructors.


🎯 Usage of this

Resolve ambiguity → Distinguish between instance variables and parameters when names clash.

Call another constructor → Use this() for constructor chaining (must be first statement).

Return current object → Enables method chaining (fluent APIs).

Pass current object → Send current object as argument to another method/constructor.

Refer to outer class → In inner classes, OuterClass.this refers to outer object.

Assign to another reference → Store current object in another variable.



⚡ Tricky Interview Questions & Notes

Q1: Why can’t you use this in a static method?

Static methods belong to the class, not an object.
this refers to the current object.
No object exists in static context → compile‑time error.

Q2: Difference between this and super?

this → current class object.
super → parent class object.
this() calls another constructor in same class; super() calls parent constructor.

Q3: Can you call two constructors using this() in one constructor?

❌ Not allowed. Only one this() or super() call, and it must be the first statement.
To chain multiple constructors, do it indirectly (constructor chaining).

Q4: Can you use this in an inner class?

Yes. this refers to inner class object.
To access outer class object → OuterClass.this.

Q5: Can you return this from a method?

✅ Yes. Used for method chaining (e.g., StringBuilder.append()).

Q6: Can you use this in a static nested class?

this refers to the nested class object itself, not the outer class.

Outer class object must be created explicitly.
